---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const breadcrumbs = [
{ label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' },
{ label: 'Signs Your Cat Is Aging', url: '/en/blog/cat-aging-signs/' }
];
const siteUrl = "https://www.petaginghub.com";
const hrefLangAlternates = [
{ lang: 'zh-TW', url: siteUrl + '/blog/cat-aging-signs' },
{ lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/cat-aging-signs' },
{ lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/blog/cat-aging-signs' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="10 Signs Your Cat Is Getting Older - Pet Aging Hub"
description="Learn the subtle and obvious signs that your cat is entering their senior years and what to do about each one."
lang="en" breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}>
<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4">
<header class="mb-8">
<span class="inline-block bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Cats</span>
<h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">10 Signs Your Cat Is Getting Older</h1>
<p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Older cats may change how they move, groom, eat, or use the litter box. Some changes accompany age, but pain and illness should never be dismissed as “just aging.” Record new changes and discuss them with a veterinarian.</p>
</header>
<div class="space-y-4">
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">1. Sleeping More Than Usual</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Sleep patterns may change with age. A marked change, especially alongside reduced appetite or play, deserves veterinary evaluation rather than an age-based assumption.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">2. Reluctance to Jump or Climb</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Less jumping or stiffness can signal discomfort, including joint pain. Ask your vet to assess the change; steps and easier access may also help. See our <a href="/en/blog/senior-cat-care/" class="text-blue-600 underline">senior cat care guide</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">3. Coat Becoming Dull or Matted</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Reduced grooming may reflect pain, limited mobility, or another problem. A newly matted or greasy coat is worth discussing with your veterinarian.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">4. Increased Thirst and Urination</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">A meaningful increase in drinking or urination needs veterinary assessment. Several conditions are possible; these signs alone cannot establish a diagnosis.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">5. Unexplained Weight Change</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Unexplained loss or gain deserves attention. Record your cat's weight over time and ask your veterinarian about a meaningful change. Our <a href="/en/blog/cat-aging-by-weight/" class="text-blue-600 underline">cat weight guide</a> adds context.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">6. Behavioral Changes</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Increased vocalization (especially at night), new aggression, confusion, or litter box changes can indicate cognitive dysfunction, pain, hyperthyroidism, or hypertension.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">7. Changes in Eyes</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Do not diagnose cloudy eyes by appearance alone. New cloudiness, bumping into objects, or sudden vision change should be assessed by a veterinarian.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">8. Dental Disease and Bad Breath</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Bad breath, chewing changes, or apparent mouth pain deserve an oral examination. Age alone cannot tell you whether dental disease is present.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">9. Changes in Eating Habits</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Eating more or less than usual, especially with weight change, can have several causes. Ask your veterinarian to investigate rather than assigning a cause at home.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">10. Disorientation and Confusion</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Disorientation or new nighttime vocalization is not a normal finding to ignore. Pain, sensory changes, and medical or cognitive conditions are among the possibilities; a veterinary evaluation helps distinguish them.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mt-8 bg-purple-50 rounded-2xl p-5 border border-purple-100">
<h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">When to Call the Vet</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">One meaningful new sign can justify a call; you do not need to wait for two. Record when it began and any accompanying changes. See <a href="/en/blog/when-to-see-vet/" class="text-blue-600 underline">when to seek veterinary care</a>; breathing difficulty, collapse, or inability to urinate requires immediate help.</p>
</div>
<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
<a href="/en/tools/symptom-checklist/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-purple-500 hover:bg-purple-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Symptom Checklist</a>
<a href="/en/blog/when-to-see-vet/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-red-100 hover:bg-red-200 text-red-800 font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">When to See the Vet</a>
<a href="/en/tools/cat-age-calculator/" class="text-blue-700 underline">Cat life-stage estimate</a>
<a href="/en/blog/senior-pet-health-guide/" class="text-blue-700 underline">Senior pet health monitoring</a>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-4">An age estimate provides general context only. It cannot establish health or explain any of the changes listed here.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-6">Sources: <a href="https://www.vet.cornell.edu/departments-centers-and-institutes/cornell-feline-health-center/health-information/feline-health-topics/special-needs-senior-cat" class="text-blue-700 underline">Cornell Feline Health Center, senior cat care</a>; <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2021-aaha-aafp-feline-life-stage-guidelines/behavior-and-environmental-needs-senior-cats/" class="text-blue-700 underline">AAHA/AAFP feline life-stage guidance</a>.</p>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
